= The <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> Slips Away

Low-ranking party members loyal to Staal have steadily climbed the ranks, until a new executive committee finally replaces the old guard that had served the party since the founding of the republic. The change is gradual, and almost gentle: the old leaders are not purged but eased into retirement, their pensions secure and their honors intact, while Staal’s men quietly take the empty chairs.

At its head now stand figures like the former Defense Minister Jarren Brask and Leland Ardwin, a nationalist voice within the Greater Worker's Guild, fiercely hostile to the Treaty of Etaluxia and far closer to Staal’s ideology than the mere pragmatism of Bristol or Leighart.

The <span style="color: #c00000;">**Marchers**</span> is not abolished, banned, or broken. It is folded into the broad tent of the <span style="color: #BFC8CC;">**GNU**</span>, with its name, press, and membership intact, where it keeps a real place as the recognized voice of the worker and no real power at all. It will go on speaking for labor in Staal's national movement, and after him, when the Commonwealth is handed up to the Empire, it will go on speaking for labor in the advisory Assembly that replaces the old chamber, a large party, sometimes the largest, in a body that decides nothing. The <span style="color: #c00000;">Reformist</span> movement does not die today, but it is made harmless.

- @spd_joever

